# Research reads latest — IRON LAW

Before stating any fact about a repo — research doc, spike, PR review, design
note, ticket comment, or chat reply — **fetch that repo and read at the remote
commit.**

A stale local checkout is not evidence. Never publish a finding caveated with
"my clone was N commits behind, re-check before implementing." Pull, re-check
yourself, then publish.

## Steps

1. `git -C <repo> fetch --all` for every repo you will cite.
2. Record branch, default branch, ahead/behind counts, and dirty state.
3. Read at `origin/<default>`, not local `HEAD`, unless the question is about a
   local or feature branch.
4. If local `HEAD` was behind, diff the missed range and say whether any cited
   file changed.
5. Re-verify non-git state — PR status, review state, ticket status — at
   publish time, not from memory.
6. Report the commit you read per repo. Keep unmerged-branch facts (branch,
   ahead/behind, PR state) separate from default-branch facts.

## Getting to latest must not modify the tree

Research is read-only work.

**Allowed:** `fetch`; `pull --ff-only origin <default>` when on the default
branch with a clean tracked tree; `git show origin/<ref>:<path>` to read.

**Never, just to read latest:** `git checkout <ref> -- .` or `-- <path>` (it
overwrites and stages the working tree — use `git show`); `rebase`, `merge`,
`stash`, `reset`, or force-push on a branch the user is working on; any write
to a repo with uncommitted tracked changes.

Rebasing a branch is a separate task the user must request; it has its own IRON
LAW in `rebase-verification.mdc`.

If you do modify a tree by mistake, say so in the same turn, restore it, and
prove the restore (branch, HEAD, clean status). Restore without asking only
when you measured the tree clean beforehand and can show nothing is at risk.
